Z||000000||Mach10||06-21-2001||08:42 PM||||I'm not doing too bad with my car at the moment. The car seems to run fine, but the short range of the car is slightly disconcerting, considering the trip I'm planning in 4 weeks. Firstly, what kind of mileage in the city should I be expecting with a well-tuned (to my knowledge. Did the plugs, wires, dist., filter, EGR valve) 1986 V6 Auto? Currently I'm getting about 156 Miles in the city. Does this sound right?<P>What kind of trouble would I have assuming I have the balls to drop the tank and attempt to put in the larger '88 gas tank? I can get my filthy mittens on one fairly easily. What's involved? Has anyone done it? Etc... Etc...||||reg||6
Z||000001||PBJ||06-21-2001||11:02 PM||||sorry but these are kms.<BR>250 kms 86 v6 4spd no a/c city<BR>180 kms 86 v6 auto with a/c<P>both are city, and a full tank of gas. both driven hard.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||06-22-2001||12:10 AM||||I remember reading somewhere that the bigger gas tank will not fit the older cars.I think the spaceframe must have been modified to accept the larger tank. I have never tried this myself.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||06-22-2001||12:22 AM||||You might also check your oxygen sensor for proper operation($20.00 and easy to change) Also check your thermostat to make sure the engine is not running too cool(rich fuel mixture). Low tire pressure, dragging brakes, and plugged up air filter can all add up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||06-22-2001||12:26 AM||||Something deffinatly sounds amiss.<BR>I've got an 87 that gets 200 city driving and thats with a bad O2 sensor. <BR>So what everyone else said. Are you getting codes of any kind? I've seen 30mpg highway on a well tuned 86-87 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||10:10 AM||||Ive been getting 200 - 210 driving 3 miles to work and other short trips on my 10 gal tank.<P>  last week I calc'd 30mpg on a partial tank refill - not sure if I click click clicked it all the way full, but 250 to 300 highway is not impossible.<P>  250 miles in the car on a trip is at least 3 hours - you should be stopping every two hours or so if for no other reason than to get the blood circulating in your netheregions.<P>  If you have found some part of the world where you can drive a Fiero and the gas stations are more than 250 miles apart, Id be interested to know where that is? If thats what you're doing then you are gonna need to carry extra gas in your trunk - not a great idea. If you are taking such a trip with other vehicles, have the other ones carry the Gerry cans.<P>   150 city on a ten gallon tank is not good BTW - O2 sensor change every 20,000 miles. Check your vacuum lines - a vacuum leak to some sensors will through the mixture way rich - for a quick check look at your tailpipes - I'll bet 10:1 they are all sooty and your exhaust smell rich when the car idles.<P>  I got interrupted while I was responding so I dont remember - does your ECM throw up any codes? if so put them up here and maybe we can figure out why you are only getting 15mph.<P>  well ok there is one way you can get 15mpg consistantly in the city - foot made of lead! if you are hotrodding all the time you pay up at the pump. If so, try running a tank or two of gas through it driving nice - it will take  a few hundred miles for the ECM to recalibrate itself to your 'new' driving style - see if your mpgs go up - you should be able to get atleast 20.<P>  its been mentined on here before - you have to pump your gas really slow to get the last 2 gallons into the tank - the nozzel will kick off at about 8 gallons - so also check - are you getting 150 miles / tank cause you aren not filling it up all the way, or are you checking the odo/gallons and getting 15mpg?<P>EDIT PS: i looked at your post - dump your engine codes and see if any are logged. I am pretty sure there are some codes that will throw the ECM into LimpHome mode and it will stay that way till the ECM is reset - one result of which could be poor mileage.<P>  there doesnt have to be anything wrong with the engine either - a sensor could glitch for a minute - register the code, and then your computer thinks that sensor is defective and will ignore it until the ECM is reset.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||06-22-2001||10:19 AM||||I used to be able to get 25 mpg with the t125 auto in my GT over the road.   But as others have said a stop every 200 miles or so (3 hours at 70 mph)is not a bad idea.  I reset my trip odometer to keep me informed as to far I've traveled.  Gas guages notoriously inaccurate.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fyerstarter||06-22-2001||11:03 AM||||1986 and older has a gas tank smaller by two gallons. I have an 86 Gt Auto and I get the same out of a tank as you do. I think it's only an 8.5 or 9 gallon tank.<p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||06-22-2001||12:34 PM||||The smallest tank used was 10.6 gallons in the 84's.  One problem is that because of the tank vent design it is very difficult to truly fill the tank.  I can slowly dribble up to three additional gallons in my 84 after the nozzle shuts off.  I can get about two more gallons in my 88 after the nozzle shuts off.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||06-22-2001||02:44 PM||||An '87 tank can be put into an '86 chassis. You have to "massage" with a BFH the lip where the upper and lower halves of the tank are joined. That lip needs to be pushed down flush with the side of the tank right where the mounting straps go over it.<P>And you will need to make/modify new mounting straps.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-22-2001||07:36 PM||||Try this as well....<P>When you fill the tank DON'T stuff it.<P>I know we've looked at that from a emissions angle and potential damage to the vapor can...<P>I've found recently that if I stuff my tank I drop to 23-24MPG from 27-28. (This tank system just passed Delaware emissions pressure test. So I'm pretty sure there's no leaks.)<P>I used to avoid stuffing the tank at all because I was often filling while working. After I changed jobs I'd click the handle once maybe twice.<P>Recently, after a couple tanks of not stuffing because I filled during lunch I found my mileage was back up. (I track my milage nearly every tank. Had to for old job.) It's stayed up since.<P>I do not know just why but it makes a diferance. It's worth giving it a try.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||06-23-2001||01:47 PM||||That makes me think... A couple of weeks ago, I had gotten the good 'ole EGR codes, and replaced the EGR. The car idles fine now... However, I didn't reset the ecm... I can just pop the ECM fuse for 10s, correct?<P>Yes, the exhaust smells rich, but I was attributing that to the fact that I had the Cat removed and a resonator installed. I'll give it a shot on m'break. My understanding is that it'll take ~10 minutes for the ECM to "learn" the timing curves and such, correct?<P>Guess I'll hop on over to PartSource and scam me an O2 sensor while I'm at it. I've given up on the gas tank thing. If I feel extremely paranoid, I'll stuff a gerry can up front with the spare, but from what I see there are plenty of stops along the way, at least until I hit the mountains. That's the tough part, but hey, I'll cross that bridge...<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000012||Mach10||06-23-2001||08:43 PM||||Do I have to drive a certain way to let my ECM Re-learn properly?||||reg||5
Z||000013||theogre||06-23-2001||09:09 PM||||No. pulling the fuse will not do it. disconnect the - battery terminal.<P>Yes. the car will run weird until it relearns things. That will take a few miles. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||06-23-2001||09:18 PM||||Thanks Ogre... So by running weird, I shouldn't push it hard, then, should I?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000015||theogre||06-23-2001||11:05 PM||||Just drive around "normal" for a day. The ECM wants to develope a long term fuel calibration as well as looking at what is happening from minute to minute. Unless there is a problem the ECM should relearn fairly quick.<P>The biggest factor in fuel economy, assuming the car doesn't have a problem, is the driver.<P>Examples...... (For everyone.)<P>When you drive the same route to work every day. you get to know the timing on redlights. When you know you aren't going to make the next light, take your foot off the gas and let inertia carry it rather than drive at/above the speed limit until the last second. (Put stick cars out of gear...)<P>Same thing entering reduced speed areas. let the car start coasting down before entering the area.<P>Avoid "punching it" off every stop. Learn to accelerate the car as smoothly as you can. This takes considerable practice with stick cars. With automatics just let the speed build without mashing the pedal.<P>Each car has a "sweet spot" in the gas pedal where you get good acceleration rate at minimal fuel consumption. Find that spot.<P>Back to stick cars.... "clean up" your shifting. Mainly shift at lower RPM points. The trick to good stick economy is finding the lowest RPM spots where you get smoothly from one gear to the next. Don't wind out a gear.<P>It sounds contrary but an automatic car often gets better mileage than stick. (We'll ignore OD for the moment. Presense or lack of OD will always skew highway mileage.) Stick gets an adavntage on highway milage but auto often does better in "city" driving. Mainly for the reasons above. It's actually quite dificult to learn to drive stick while you optimize the fuel economy and performance. An auto is most often optimized for city driving. Once the automatic's TCC is locked you'll get almost as good highway  mileage as stick but you do loose a bit due to higher rotating mass.<P>In all seriousness... try not cramming every last drop into the fuel tank. I'm still investigating this but so far I gained 4MPG on average by not stuffing the tank.<P>It's something to try anyway.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||06-24-2001||03:26 AM||||I assure you, ogre, that I baby my baby. I drive it nice and smooth, unleashing the beast only to administer swift anal justice to any ricer foolish enough to cut my faded ass off. Asside from that, I drive calmly, about 5km over speed limit, and know most of the lights in my area.<P>I reset the ECM, and got some truly neat stuff happening with the engine for the first 10kms... After that, smooth as butter, at least until I let the damn thing idle for 5 minutes. Engine code lights up, turns out to be 44 --lean mixture--. WTF?!? Sooty tailpipe, stinky exhaust, sounds rich to me. Am I on the right track thinking that the o2 sensor might be ferschnickered? See post "Increased Range Part II - The Sequel"||||reg||8
